---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import GpaCalculator from '@components/islands/GpaCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('everyday', 'gpa-calculator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How is GPA calculated?', answer: 'Each letter grade is worth a number of grade points (A = 4.0, B = 3.0, and so on). Multiply each course\'s grade points by its credit hours, add those products up, and divide by the total credit hours. This calculator does it automatically as you add courses.' },
  { question: 'What is the 4.0 grade scale?', answer: 'A+ is 4.3, A is 4.0, A− is 3.7, B+ is 3.3, B is 3.0, and it continues down in steps to D− at 0.7 and F at 0.0. P (pass), NP (not pass), I (incomplete) and W (withdrawal) carry no grade points and are ignored rather than scored as zero. Some schools cap an A+ at 4.0 or run their pluses and minuses differently, so check your institution\'s official scale.' },
  { question: 'How do P, NP, I and W affect my GPA?', answer: 'They do not. A pass, a not-pass, an incomplete and a withdrawal carry no grade points, so those courses are left out of both the total credits and the average entirely. That is different from an F, which is scored as 0 grade points and does pull the average down.' },
  { question: 'What does the GPA Planning Calculator do?', answer: 'It works backwards from a target. Give it your current GPA and the credits it covers, the GPA you want, and how many credits you have left, and it returns the minimum average those remaining credits need to reach. If the target cannot be reached even with the highest grade on the scale, it says so rather than returning a number you could never hit.' },
  { question: 'What is a good GPA?', answer: 'On a 4.0 scale, 3.0 is generally considered good (roughly a B average), 3.5+ is strong, and 3.8+ is excellent. Many scholarships and graduate programs set 3.0 as a minimum, but "good" ultimately depends on your school, major and goals.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between weighted and unweighted GPA?', answer: 'An unweighted GPA caps every course at 4.0. A weighted GPA gives harder classes a boost — typically +0.5 for honors and +1.0 for AP or IB — so a weighted GPA can exceed 4.0 (often up to 5.0). This tool uses the standard unweighted 4.0 scale.' },
  { question: 'How do credit hours affect my GPA?', answer: 'GPA is weighted by credits, so higher-credit courses count more. A top grade in a 4-credit course moves your GPA more than the same grade in a 1-credit course — and a poor grade in a heavy course hurts more.' },
  { question: 'How do I calculate my cumulative GPA across semesters?', answer: 'Add up the grade points (grade × credits) from every course across all semesters, then divide by your total credit hours. A cumulative GPA is not the average of each semester\'s GPA unless every semester carried the same number of credits.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  rail
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-03' }}
  intro="Add each course's credits and grade for your GPA and a full grade-point report, then use the planning calculator below to work out the GPA your future courses need."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <GpaCalculator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to calculate your GPA</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Add a <strong>row for each course</strong> you want to include.</li>
      <li>Pick the <strong>letter grade</strong> from the list and enter the course's <strong>credit hours</strong>.</li>
      <li>Add more rows as needed; the credit-weighted GPA updates from every course at once.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      A course with zero credits is accepted but does not move the average — handy for a
      pass/fail class you want to list without counting. You need at least one course carrying
      credit hours before the tool can return a GPA.
    </p>

    <h2>Letter grade and the numerical equivalents used for this calculator</h2>
    <p>
      Grade point average is the average of the grades you attained in each course, taking course
      credit into account. Grading systems vary between countries and even between schools; this
      calculator translates letter grades into the numerical values below.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Letter grade</th><th>Grade points</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A+</td><td>4.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A</td><td>4</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A−</td><td>3.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B+</td><td>3.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B</td><td>3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>B−</td><td>2.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C+</td><td>2.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C</td><td>2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>C−</td><td>1.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>D+</td><td>1.3</td></tr>
        <tr><td>D</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>D−</td><td>0.7</td></tr>
        <tr><td>F</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>P (pass), NP (not pass), I (incomplete), W (withdrawal)</td><td>ignored</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Most schools, colleges and universities in the United States use a system based on the letters
      above, though E is sometimes used instead of F. Systems do differ on what constitutes an A or
      a B, and some do not include an A+ or a B−. Others weight harder courses more heavily, so
      whatever grade is attained there has a larger effect on the overall GPA; this calculator
      accounts for that through the number of credits attributed to a course, where credit is the
      course's weighting.
    </p>
    <p>
      The four ignored grades are ignored, not zeroed. A withdrawal contributes no credits and no
      grade points, so it leaves your average exactly where it was — which is a different outcome
      from an F, scored as 0 and averaged in like any other grade.
    </p>

    <h2>Planning ahead</h2>
    <p>
      The <strong>GPA Planning Calculator</strong> answers the other question: not what your GPA is,
      but what it has to become. Give it your current GPA and the credits behind it, your target,
      and the credits still to come, and it returns the minimum average those credits need.
    </p>
    <p>
      For a current GPA of 3.663 over 8 credits, a target of 3 and 15 credits still to come, the
      next 15 credits need to average <strong>2.646</strong> or higher. If a target cannot be reached
      even by scoring the highest grade on the scale across every remaining credit, the calculator
      says so instead of returning a number you could never hit.
    </p>

    <h2>The formula</h2>
    <p>
      GPA is a credit-weighted average, not a plain one. For each course, multiply its grade
      points by its credit hours to get that course's <strong>quality points</strong>; add the
      quality points across all courses, then divide by the total credit hours:
    </p>
    <p><strong>GPA = total quality points ÷ total credit hours</strong></p>
    <p>
      Weighting by credits is why a heavier course pulls harder: a strong grade in a 4-credit
      class moves your GPA more than the same grade in a 1-credit class, and a poor grade in a
      heavy course hurts more.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>Suppose you took three courses this term:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A (4.0) × 4 credits = 16.0 quality points</li>
      <li>B+ (3.3) × 3 credits = 9.9 quality points</li>
      <li>B− (2.7) × 3 credits = 8.1 quality points</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      That is <strong>34.0 quality points</strong> across <strong>10 credit hours</strong>, so
      the GPA is 34.0 ÷ 10 = <strong>3.40</strong>. Notice it is not the simple average of 4.0,
      3.3 and 2.7 (which would be 3.33) — the extra weight on the 4-credit A nudges it upward.
    </p>

    <h2>Weighted vs. unweighted GPA</h2>
    <p>This calculator uses the <strong>unweighted</strong> 4.0 scale, where every course maxes out at 4.0. Many high schools also report a <strong>weighted</strong> GPA that rewards course difficulty — commonly +0.5 for honors and +1.0 for AP or IB classes — which is why some transcripts show GPAs above 4.0. Colleges often recalculate to their own scale, so know which number a given form is asking for.</p>

    <h2>Cumulative GPA across semesters</h2>
    <p>
      A cumulative GPA uses the same method over every course you have taken: add up the quality
      points from all terms and divide by all the credit hours. It is <em>not</em> the average
      of each semester's GPA unless every semester carried exactly the same number of credits.
      To roll several terms together here, enter each course as its own row.
    </p>

    <h2>What counts as a good GPA</h2>
    <p>As a rough guide, 3.0 is a solid B average, 3.5+ is competitive for many programs, and 3.8+ is excellent. Context matters more than the number, though: a strong GPA in a demanding major can outweigh a higher one in an easier track. To work out an individual course grade from your assignments and exams, use the <a href="/everyday/grade-calculator">grade calculator</a>. This uses the standard unweighted 4.0 scale; your institution's scale may differ.</p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
